{"candidates":[{"claim":"A test that pins an exact artifact count violates the Open–Closed Principle (OCP): the module under test should be open to accepting new evidence (extension) but closed to arbitrary modification. Instead of asserting N == fixed_count, the test should assert an invariant that holds across all legitimate extensions — e.g., every committed artifact satisfies validation, provenance, and integrity constraints, and the count remains within a domain-defined boundary.","counterconditions":["If the system is a closed-world registry where no new artifacts are ever legitimately admitted, exact-count assertion may be appropriate","If the invariant is too weak (e.g., 'count > 0'), it may pass even when most artifacts are corrupt","If legitimate new evidence can itself be malformed and still be committed, the invariant must include validation of the evidence-acceptance pathway, not just the committed artifact"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: new evidence arrives as a legitimate, well-formed extension to the artifact set. The exact-count assertion has no causal link to correctness — it only detects whether mutation occurred, conflating expected extension with unexpected corruption. An invariant-based assertion causally targets the property that actually matters: each artifact's validity. When new evidence arrives, the invariant still holds because the evidence is legitimate; when corruption occurs, the invariant breaks because the artifact is malformed, regardless of count.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.92},{"claim":"The test should assert an existential property — 'for every committed artifact, P(artifact) holds' — rather than a universal cardinality pin. This aligns with the Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) extended to data: subtypes of the artifact set (including newly admitted evidence) must satisfy the contracts of the supertype. The supporting testing principle is contract testing over state snapshot testing.","counterconditions":["If admission pathways can be bypassed, the universal P assertion may pass on pre-existing valid artifacts while newly admitted invalid ones are only caught if the test iterates the full set","If P is trivially true for all artifacts (e.g., 'artifact exists'), the test provides no discriminative power","If the committed set is lazily evaluated or paginated, the test must ensure it materializes the complete set before asserting the universal quantifier"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: each artifact enters the committed set through a controlled admission pathway. If that pathway enforces invariants, then any artifact passing admission necessarily satisfies P. The count is epiphenomenal — it changes as a consequence of admission events, not as a guard of correctness. Asserting P over the universal set causally verifies that the admission pathway functioned correctly for each member; asserting count==N only verifies that the net number of admission and removal events matched a fixed expectation, which has no causal bearing on individual artifact validity.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"claim":"Following Bertrand Meyer's Design by Contract, the test should assert the class invariant of the artifact collection — properties that hold before and after every public operation — rather than a snapshot equality. This is supported by the principle that tests should verify behavioral contracts (postconditions and invariants), not implementation-state snapshots, which couples tests to incidental representation choices.","counterconditions":["If the class invariant includes a cardinality constraint (e.g., 'exactly one canonical artifact per key'), then an exact-count assertion may actually be the correct invariant","If the testing framework cannot express or check class invariants natively, the invariant must be manually encoded, introducing its own error surface","If the invariant is too broad, it may be computationally expensive to verify on every operation, creating practical pressure to weaken the test"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: a class invariant is established by the constructor and preserved by every public method (the constructor-effect + method-preservation contract). New evidence arrival is a public operation that, if correctly implemented, preserves the invariant. The exact-count assertion breaks because it asserts a stronger property than the invariant — it requires no net change, which is not guaranteed by the contract. The invariant (e.g., 'all artifacts have unique IDs and valid provenance chains') is causally preserved through correct admission; the count is merely one dimension that can vary within the invariant's tolerance.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"The test should assert a monotonicity property: the committed artifact set only grows through legitimate admission or shrinks through legitimate withdrawal, with every transition preserving per-artifact integrity. This aligns with the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): a test for evidence-admission correctness should assert the admission contract (monotonic, validated growth), not the incidental state-count contract, which is a separate concern belonging to a capacity or resource test.","counterconditions":["If the system legitimately allows non-monotonic reordering or replacement (not just growth/shrinkage), monotonicity alone is insufficient","If the test framework cannot capture transition events (only snapshots), monotonicity must be inferred by comparing consecutive snapshots, which may miss intermediate invalid states that are later corrected","If 'legitimate admission' is not crisply defined, the monotonicity assertion degenerates into 'the set changed,' which provides no correctness guarantee"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: the artifact set transitions state through discrete events (admission, withdrawal). Correctness of the set is not defined by a fixed point in state-space but by the legality of each transition. A monotonicity assertion causally targets the transition function: every admitted artifact passed validation. An exact-count assertion targets a single state coordinate (cardinality), which changes legitimately under transitions and thus cannot serve as a correctness oracle. The monotonicity+integrity assertion discriminates correct transitions from corrupt ones because corrupt transitions admit invalid artifacts or remove valid ones without authorization.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"claim":"The test should assert a bounded-range property — count is within [min, max] where min captures the known baseline and max is a domain-defined ceiling — combined with a per-artifact validity check. This is supported by the principle of robust testing (tests should tolerate expected variation) and the Interface Segregation Principle (ISP): consumers of the artifact set should not depend on its exact size but on the validity and availability of artifacts within the interface contract.","counterconditions":["If max is set too high, the test becomes a near-tautology that catches only catastrophic overflow","If the baseline min is itself stale (e.g., derived from a previous snapshot), the range may exclude legitimate evidence that replaced deprecated artifacts, causing a false failure","If evidence arrival is bursty and can exceed any fixed max, the bound becomes an artificial constraint that causes false failures — the test then encodes a resource-limit policy, not a correctness property"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: the count varies as a causal consequence of evidence-arrival events, which are stochastic or exogenously triggered. An exact pin assumes the count is deterministic, which is causally false in an open-world system. A bounded-range assertion causally models the true generative process: count = baseline + f(evidence_events), where f is monotonically non-decreasing and bounded by domain constraints. The per-artifact validity check provides the correctness content that the range alone lacks. Together they assert 'the set is non-empty, bounded, and every member is valid,' which is the actual causal contract.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A chemical equilibrium has a thermodynamic driving force (energy minimization) that determines the equilibrium; an artifact set has no analogous objective function — 'correctness' is a human-defined contract, not a physical law","Chemical equilibrium can be characterized by a continuous potential; artifact-set membership is discrete and combinatorial","The chemical system's dynamics are governed by known rate laws; the artifact admission pathway may be arbitrary code with no closed-form model"],"overturn_conditions":["If the artifact system is genuinely closed-world (no new evidence ever), the equilibrium analogy fails and exact-count pinning may be correct","If the admission pathway is trivially correct (e.g., no validation possible), runtime checking adds cost without discriminative power","If the domain admits a formal proof that the count is invariant (e.g., a bijection argument), the snapshot assertion is provably equivalent to the invariant and the runtime approach is unnecessary"],"shared_structure":["A process generates a sequence of states through discrete transitions","Correctness is a property of each transition's postcondition, not of any single state snapshot","Pinning a state coordinate (exact count / exact equilibrium) breaks on legitimate state change","The correct assertion is a runtime or property-based check on the transition function, not a snapshot equality"],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["Just as pinning a reaction's exact equilibrium concentration breaks when temperature changes (a legitimate perturbation), pinning an exact artifact count breaks when evidence arrives (a legitimate extension)","The correct test in both cases is to assert the invariant that holds across the perturbation: the reaction quotient relationship in chemistry, the per-artifact validity contract in software","Runtime assertion on the admission event mirrors continuous monitoring of reaction rate and composition rather than a single-point titration"]},"claim":"Radical atypical alternative: no static test assertion can correctly pin artifact-set properties in an open-world system because correctness is fundamentally a property of the admission process, not the admitted set. The test should be replaced by a runtime invariant checker (a continuous assertion or property-based test) that fires on every admission event, verifying the transition function's postcondition. This follows from the Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP): depend on the abstraction (admission contract), not the concretion (snapshot of admitted artifacts). The published principle is property-based testing (QuickCheck-style) over the transition relation, not example-based snapshot testing.","counterconditions":["If the admission pathway is not instrumentable (e.g., a third-party system), a runtime checker cannot be inserted and the approach is infeasible","If the runtime checker has a performance cost that is unacceptable in production, it must be conditionally enabled, which creates a gap in coverage","If the postcondition itself is underspecified, the runtime checker enforces an arbitrary constraint and becomes a new source of false failures","Property-based testing requires a shrinking strategy for the artifact generator; if the artifact domain is complex, shrinking may not converge to minimal counterexamples, reducing diagnostic value"],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Causal mechanism: in an open-world system, the committed set is the output of a potentially unbounded sequence of admission events. Any static assertion at a point in time samples a single state from this trajectory and cannot causally distinguish a correct trajectory that happens to be at a high count from an incorrect one that happens to match the pinned count. A runtime checker attached to the admission pathway causally intercepts each transition and verifies its postcondition, making the correctness check co-extensive with the generative process itself. The causal link is direct: admission event → checker fires → postcondition verified or violation raised, all in the same causal chain.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.18}],"simulation_proposals":[]}